# Code quality

**Canonical source:** [docs/CODE-QUALITY.md](../../docs/CODE-QUALITY.md)

Read it before non-trivial structural work: a new module, a new abstraction, changing a boundary, or adding a dependency direction. Apply its standards to any code you touch, even when the task is something else.

This is an index of its sections so you can recognize which one applies — read the doc for the actual standard, examples, and rationale.

**A. Structure and design** — layer boundaries and dependency direction; SOLID for this codebase; function/class/component size budgets; which Gang of Four patterns are in deliberate use (and which are deliberately not).

**B. Types and state** — make illegal states unrepresentable; separate authored (user-entered) from derived (calculated) data; immutability by default.

**C. Clarity** — clean naming and comments that explain constraints, not history; one source of truth per fact; ubiquitous language (see `terminology-compliance.mdc` for the vocabulary itself).

**D. Failure** — one error convention (`Result` for expected failure, `throw` for programmer error); every async operation has a named loading/success/failure resolution.

**E. Testing** — behavior over implementation; characterization tests before refactoring risky code; test data builders over inline fixtures; coverage as a ratchet. Mechanics live in `testing-vitest.mdc`, including the Humble Object rule.

**F. Boundaries and enforcement** — module boundaries backed by lint rules, not just habit; accessibility as a standing requirement; ADRs for decisions worth explaining in six months.

**G. Code smell catalogue** — named smells this codebase is prone to (long function, god object, magic number, mixed concerns, shotgun surgery, speculative generality, stale comment, etc.) and the fix for each.

For a full review of the whole codebase against every standard here, use the `code-quality-review` skill rather than doing this ad hoc.

When a standard changes, update `docs/CODE-QUALITY.md` in the same change set. Do not edit only this index or let it diverge from the doc's sections.
